# Gardiner–Lakic, *Quasiconformal Teichmüller Theory* (2000)

F. P. Gardiner, N. Lakic, *Quasiconformal Teichmüller Theory*, Mathematical Surveys and Monographs 76, American
Mathematical Society, Providence 2000. Statement-level formalization: genuine definitions, every result of the book
stated with a `sorry` proof; results already in Mathlib, the project or the References are passed with an `exists:`
pointer in the file's item index.

## Sources

The Mathpix text `teichmuller.txt` of `Quasiconformal Teichmuller Theory (…) (Z-Library)\33219a5e-…` (14 238 lines);
line numbers `l.` in the files refer to it. Doubtful formulas, misprints and the lost Figure 3.1 were checked against the
second OCR with page images, `…(Z-Library)8\full.md` (Ch. 1–8, cited `GL8:<line>`) and `…(Z-Library)1\full.md`
(Ch. 9–18, cited `GL1:<line>`).

## Coverage

Delivered so far: Chapter 1 *Quasiconformal mapping* (§1.2–§1.7; §1.1 has no items), Chapter 2 *Riemann surfaces*
(§2.1–§2.10), Chapter 3 *Quadratic differentials, part I* (§3.1–§3.10 and the Appendix), Chapter 4 *Quadratic
differentials, part II* (§4.1–§4.12), Chapter 5 *Teichmüller equivalence* (§5.1–§5.4), Chapter 6 *The Bers embedding*
(§6.1–§6.7), Chapter 7 *Kobayashi's metric on Teichmüller space* (§7.1–§7.4), Chapter 8 *Isomorphisms and
automorphisms* (§8.1–§8.9), Chapter 9 *Teichmüller uniqueness* (§9.1–§9.6), Chapter 10 *The mapping class group*
(§10.1–§10.5), Chapter 11 *Jenkins–Strebel differentials* (§11.1–§11.7), Chapter 12 *Measured foliations*
(§12.1–§12.7), Chapter 13 *Obstacle problems* (§13.1–§13.9), Chapter 14 *Asymptotic Teichmüller space*
(§14.1–§14.11), Chapter 15 *Asymptotically extremal maps* (§15.1–§15.4), Chapter 16 *Universal Teichmüller
space* (§16.1–§16.8), Chapter 17 *Substantial boundary points* (§17.1–§17.7) and Chapter 18 *Earthquake
mappings* (§18.1–§18.5), one file per section,
in book order (chapter introductions are merged into the first section file;
the proof-only sections §13.7 and §14.6 are merged into the files of §13.6 and §14.5). Exercises are not items.

## Deferred

- §11.6 (Chapter 11): the example surface `R` with its differential `φ` and properties (a)–(d) (l. 8653–8693), and
  Proposition 11.1 (l. 8698). Stating them faithfully needs the surface `R̂` built as a quotient of the unit square
  with charts `(z − a_k)²`, i.e. definitions with proof obligations, which the no-proof rule excludes; the example
  is used nowhere else in the book (leader calibration of Part C, C0.5). Definition 11.3 is stated
  (`DecaysGeometrically`).
- §13.5 (Chapter 13): the variation surfaces `S_ε` and `S_{μ(ε)}` as objects (constructions replaced by
  characterizing predicates, `IsSchifferVariation`, `IsBeltramiVariation`; leader calibration of Part D, D0.6).
  Lemma 13.3 and Theorem 13.5 are stated.

## Standing conventions and their modelling (leader calibration of Part A, rulings A0.1–A0.8)

- **Riemann surfaces** are Mathlib charted spaces with the bundle of `References.IsRiemannSurface` (shared layer),
  as in LiZhongQC §22; quasiconformal maps of surfaces are `LiZhongQC.IsKQCRS`. Surfaces in existential positions and
  quotients are bundled as `RSBundle`.
- **Fuchsian groups** are subgroups of SL(2,ℝ) with `LiZhongQC.IsFuchsian` (the book's PSL(2,ℝ) groups modulo `±1`);
  the book's disc model of §3.1–§3.2 is read in the ℍ model (the Cayley map is an isometry of all the spaces
  involved); the Poincaré series over the Möbius maps is `thetaGL`.
- **Plane quasiconformal maps** are `LiZhongQC.IsKQC` (the geometric definition, Definition 1.4); the analytic
  Definition 1.2 is `IsKQCAnalytic`. Normalized solutions of the Beltrami equation are the project's
  `LiZhongQC.normalizedSolution`.
- **Spaces of differentials** are carrier-level sets (`integrableHolQDSet`, `aSpaceClosed`, …) with explicit norms;
  duals `Z(·)` are sets of functionals restricted by `IsBoundedFunctionalOn`. `T(R)` is a `Quot` of `M(R)`; the
  boundary condition of Definition 2.6 is read through the Fuchsian lift (the lifted homotopy fixes `ℝ̂`).
- **Plane readings**: extremal length (§2.3–§2.4) and the Poincaré density (2.18) are stated for plane domains; the
  holomorphic dependence on `μ` (Theorems 1.1, 1.2) is read weakly (through the pairing with `L¹`).
- **Hyperbolic metric**: the book's curvature −4 normalization (`Gehring1978.hypDensity`,
  `LiZhongQC.discPoincareDist`, `poincareDensityGL`).
- **Chapters 4–7** (leader calibration of Part B, rulings B0.1–B0.8): Ch. 4 works on an abstract `R` (finite
  analytic type `IsFiniteAnalyticType`; from §4.6 the classes of `TeichSpace R p`); Chapters 6–7 and Theorem 4.14 use
  the Fuchsian model (`LiZhongQC.BeltramiGamma`, `LiZhongQC.TeichGamma`), linked to `TeichSpace` in Ch. 5
  (`para5_2_teichGamma_equiv`). No instances on Teichmüller spaces: openness, charts and holomorphy are explicit
  (`IsHolBMap`, `IsBanachAtlasT`, `holDiscsT`, `holDiscsM`). The cusp norm `cuspNorm` has the curvature −4
  normalization; Kobayashi's pseudometric `kobayashiPseudo` is generic over a set of holomorphic discs.
- **Chapters 8–11** (leader calibration of Part C, rulings C0.1–C0.8): statements about `T` as a space (§8.1–§8.3,
  §8.5, Ch. 10, §11.7) use the Fuchsian model `LiZhongQC.TeichGamma`; a map of `T` is holomorphic when it maps
  `holDiscsT` to `holDiscsT` (`IsHolAutT`), the geometric isomorphism `ρ_h` is the relation `IsRhoGamma`, and the
  mapping class groups are quotients (`mcgGamma`, `mcgRS`). Ch. 9, the isometries of `A(R)` (§8.3–§8.9) and
  §11.1–§11.5 are on the surface level (`integrableHolQDSet`, `TeichSpace R p`); closed curves are
  `LiZhongQC.Loop` with `LiZhongQC.FreelyHomotopic`.
- **Chapters 12–14** (leader calibration of Part D, rulings D0.1–D0.10): Ch. 12–13 work on surfaces of finite
  analytic type (`IsFiniteAnalyticType`); the finite topological type of Ch. 13 is read on the double with its
  anticonformal involution (`IsJSymmetricQD`). Measured foliations are a predicate (`IsMeasuredFoliation`) and
  measure equivalence is equality of heights (`AreMeasureEquiv`); heights and widths of quadratic differentials
  are `qdHeight`, `qdWidth`; the surface extremal length is `extremalLengthLoopRS`. The obstacle family `𝓕` carries
  a quasiconformal `g : S → S_f` for the homotopy class (`IsObstacleFamilyMember`). Ch. 14: `AT(R)` is a `Quot`
  (`AsymTeichSpace`), `T₀` is `H ≤ 1` (`IsInT0`); in the Fuchsian and quasi-Fuchsian models `b*(ν, G)` is
  `bStarOmega` and `B₀` is `boundedCuspForms0`; configurations `Ω, C, Ω*` are normalized by `∞ ∈ C`
  (`IsQCConfiguration`).
- **Chapters 15–18** (leader calibration of Part E, rulings E0.1–E0.10): Ch. 15 on the surface level as §14.8–14.11
  (weighted norms `weightedNormInf`, `weightedNormL1`; `IsAsymExtremal`). Ch. 16: `T(Δ)`, `T₀(Δ)`, `AT(Δ)` are the
  surface-level spaces of `LiZhongQC.unitDiscOpens`; quasisymmetric circle maps are Pommerenke's
  `IsSensePreservingCircleHomeo` + `IsQuasisymmetricA`, with the constant-bearing `IsMCondition`; `Φ` is the
  relation `IsPhiQS`; partial topological groups are generic (`IsPartialTopGroupSystem`); quasicircles are
  normalized through `∞` and welding uses Riemann maps with boundary maps (`IsWeldingMap`); the disc template is
  read from Figure 16.2 of the second OCR. Ch. 17 on plane domains: `T(Ω)` is frontier equivalence of normalized
  solutions (`teichRelPlane`), local dilatations `hpStar`, `hp`, `bp`, `betaP`. Ch. 18: finite and general
  earthquakes are characterized, not constructed (`IsFiniteLeftEarthquake` through the shears `leftShift`,
  `IsLeftEarthquake` on the disc with `hypLine`); earthquake measures are pairs of signed measures (lines between
  finite points, lines to `∞`) with Thurston's norm `earthquakeMeasureNorm`. The "manifold / tangent space /
  almost complex structure" theorems (Cor 16.1, Thms 16.3, 16.5–16.8, 18.4) are stated as the concrete
  bijections, curves and bounds of their proofs.
- **Cross-ratio norm** (§3.6–§3.10): quadruples of distinct finite points of `Λ`; quadruples with `∞` appear through
  the three-point form `crDistortionInf` where the book uses them.

